#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Prove Gemini CLI deny/rewrite gate execution end-to-end against harmless fake targets.

The adapter contract (docs/notes/harness-adapter-contract.md §6.4) ships a
Gemini CLI `BeforeTool` adapter, and its tests pin the envelopes it renders --
but unit tests assert what OUR code emits, not what Gemini CLI's harness does
with it. This script is the missing half: it plays Gemini CLI's side of the
wire, built strictly from the published hook documentation, and proves by
execution that a deny stops a harmless fake target while the session continues
after the denial, that an allow passes through, that a rewrite's replacement
input is what actually runs, and that an unmodeled/MCP tool name never routes
to ICG at all.

Gemini CLI is not installed here (and its hooks fire inside a live model
session), so the harness-side interpretation is implemented from the official
documentation (re-checked 2026-09-20):

  docs/hooks/reference.md in github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli
    - `settings.json` carries a `hooks` object; each event (here `BeforeTool`)
      is an array of hook definition groups: {matcher?, sequential?, hooks:
      [{type: "command", command, timeout?}]}. `matcher` is a REGEX compared
      against the tool name; built-in tools are named like `run_shell_command`,
      `write_file`, `replace`, `read_file`; MCP tools follow the `mcp__`
      naming pattern. `timeout` is in MILLISECONDS, default 60000.
    - Input (stdin JSON): session_id, transcript_path, cwd, hook_event_name,
      timestamp, plus the per-event tool_name, tool_input (object), optional
      mcp_context (MCP tool calls) and original_request_name.
    - Exit 0: success -- stdout is parsed as the decision JSON ("preferred for
      all logic"), and "silence is mandatory": stdout must carry exactly one
      JSON object, nothing else.
    - Exit 2: system block; stderr becomes the rejection reason; the turn
      continues.
    - Any other exit, a crash, or a timeout: a WARNING, non-fatal, the CLI
      continues. The schema has no fail-closed knob at all -- Gemini's
      dispatch is natively fail-open (the installer therefore writes a
      bounded `timeout` only).
    - decision: "deny" (alias "block") prevents the tool call; reason is
      REQUIRED when denied and is sent to the agent as the tool error,
      "allowing it to respond or retry" -- a denial is non-fatal for the
      session, which is property (b) below.
    - hookSpecificOutput.tool_input is an object that MERGES WITH AND
      OVERRIDES the model's arguments field-by-field before execution --
      the rewrite channel. ICG renders the complete replacement and lets
      Gemini CLI merge it.

What gets proven here, against the SHIPPED packs (not a synthetic one) and
harmless fake targets only:

  S0  The dispatch interpretation itself, self-checked against stub hooks
      (deny, the "block" alias, the permissive empty object, exit 2, exit 3,
      the millisecond timeout, unparsable stdout, the tool_input
      merge-override, and the two foreign-envelope controls that make ICG's
      `decision` spelling load-bearing), so the ICG scenarios below are
      known to run through faithful semantics.
  S1  BeforeTool DENY: `bao kv put ... password=hunter2` (the openbao
      inline-secret-literal rule) wrapping a marker-creating redirection.
      The deny means the command never reaches a shell: the marker file
      does not exist afterwards and the bao shim is never invoked.
  S2  The session continues after the denial (b) and an allow passes
      through (c): the follow-up call in the SAME session_id is still
      gated -- the hook answers again -- allowed by the permissive empty
      object, and executes. The denial was a tool error for the agent,
      not a dead session and not a dead gate.
  S3  BeforeTool REWRITE (d): `git push --force origin main` in a
      deliberately diverged scratch clone. hookSpecificOutput.tool_input
      carries the complete replacement input; the documented
      merge-override yields `git push origin main` as the command that
      actually runs (PATH-shim ground truth, non-fast-forward rejection,
      baseline commit survives on the diverged scratch remote), and the
      input's other fields survive the merge intact.
  S4  Matcher scoping (e): with the installer's entry as the ONLY
      BeforeTool configuration, unmodeled tool names (MCP
      `mcp__fs__write_file` and `mcp_fs_write_file`, read-only `read_file`,
      `google_web_search`, and the near-collisions `safe_write_file` /
      `replace_all`) select ZERO hooks -- ICG is never invoked and the call
      proceeds -- while each of the three modeled names selects exactly the
      ICG hook. A search-semantics control shows an UNANCHORED `write_file`
      matcher would have routed the MCP names to ICG: the anchors are
      load-bearing.
  S5  Hygiene: verdict-shaped telemetry only, the denial recorded in the
      operational log with the command text redacted, every artifact under
      the scratch tree.

The configuration the dispatch runs against is produced by the REAL
installer (`icg install-gemini-hooks`) against a scratch project seeded with
unrelated settings and an unrelated event array, and the dispatcher runs the
hook command exactly as it reads it from the installed settings.json -- the
proof exercises the installer's output, not a hand-built copy of it.

Every artifact lives under one mktemp -d scratch tree (under /tmp) which is
removed on success (all targets harmless, nothing left behind) and
deliberately left on failure for diagnosis. Hook-side state (telemetry,
denial log, session state, health) is redirected into the scratch tree so
the run never touches the host's live caches. Exit code is 0 only when every
assertion held.

Usage:
  scripts/gemini-dispatch-e2e [--icg-bin PATH] [--keep] [--repo-root DIR]

  --icg-bin     skip the build and drive this binary instead
  --keep        keep the scratch tree on success for inspection
  --repo-root   defaults to the parent of this script's directory
"""

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
from pathlib import Path

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gemini CLI's documented dispatch semantics
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#: The installed BeforeTool matcher (src/gemini_hooks.rs): anchored to the
#: three tool names the adapter models. The anchors are the scoping
#: guarantee under test in S4.
INSTALLED_MATCHER = "^(run_shell_command|write_file|replace)$"

#: Denial decisions Gemini's hook reader acts on. "block" is a documented
#: alias of "deny"; a top-level decision: "allow" is NOT part of the deny
#: set -- and ICG never emits one (its BeforeTool impact is unspecified).
DENY_DECISIONS = {"deny", "block"}


class Decision:
    """One hook run interpreted the way Gemini CLI's docs say to interpret it."""

    def __init__(self, proceeds, reason="", tool_input_override=None, detail=""):
        self.proceeds = proceeds
        self.reason = reason
        self.tool_input_override = tool_input_override
        self.detail = detail

    def as_dict(self):
        out = {"proceeds": self.proceeds, "interpretation": self.detail}
        if self.reason:
            out["reason"] = self.reason
        if self.tool_input_override is not None:
            out["tool_input_override"] = self.tool_input_override
        return out


def interpret_before_tool(rc, stdout, stderr, timed_out):
    """Interpret one BeforeTool command hook result per the docs.

    Returns a Decision. This is the load-bearing function of the proof: it
    encodes the three documented outcomes -- exit 0 (stdout parsed as the
    decision JSON), exit 2 (block, stderr as the rejection reason), and
    everything else (non-fatal warning, the CLI continues). There is no
    fail-closed knob in Gemini's schema, so no failure can ever become a
    block here; the only blocking documents are a decision deny/block
    carrying its required reason, or an exit 2.
    """
    if timed_out or rc is None or rc < 0 or rc not in (0, 2):
        # Crash (signal), timeout, or any non-0/non-2 exit: a documented
        # non-fatal warning -- the CLI continues with the tool call.
        failure = f"hook failure (rc={rc}, timed_out={timed_out})"
        return Decision(True, detail=f"{failure}; a documented non-fatal warning -- the CLI continues")
    if rc == 2:
        return Decision(
            False,
            reason=stderr.strip(),
            detail="exit 2 blocks; stderr is the rejection reason and the turn continues",
        )
    # Exit 0: stdout is the decision document -- exactly one JSON object.
    text = stdout.strip()
    try:
        doc = json.loads(text) if text else None
    except json.JSONDecodeError as error:
        return Decision(
            True,
            detail=(
                f"exit 0 but stdout is not JSON ({error}); the docs define no "
                "decision for this and there is no fail-closed knob -- "
                "non-fatal warning, the call proceeds"
            ),
        )
    if doc is None:
        return Decision(
            True,
            detail=(
                "exit 0 with silent stdout; the docs demand silence but define "
                "no decision for it -- non-fatal warning, the call proceeds"
            ),
        )
    if not isinstance(doc, dict):
        return Decision(
            True,
            detail=(
                f"exit 0 stdout is a {type(doc).__name__}, not an object; no "
                "decision to read -- non-fatal warning, the call proceeds"
            ),
        )
    decision = doc.get("decision")
    if decision in DENY_DECISIONS:
        reason = doc.get("reason")
        if not isinstance(reason, str) or not reason:
            return Decision(
                False,
                detail=(
                    f"decision {decision!r} without the required reason -- the "
                    "call is blocked with an empty tool error"
                ),
            )
        return Decision(
            False,
            reason=reason,
            detail=(
                f'decision "{decision}" blocks the call; reason goes to the '
                "agent as the tool error and the turn continues"
            ),
        )
    override = None
    nested = doc.get("hookSpecificOutput")
    if isinstance(nested, dict) and isinstance(nested.get("tool_input"), dict):
        override = nested["tool_input"]
    detail = "no deny decision on stdout; the call proceeds"
    if override is not None:
        detail += (
            " with hookSpecificOutput.tool_input merging over the model's arguments"
        )
    return Decision(True, tool_input_override=override, detail=detail)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Evidence plumbing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

CHECKS = []


def check(label, condition, detail=""):
    CHECKS.append((label, bool(condition)))
    print(f"    [{'PASS' if condition else 'FAIL'}] {label}" + (f" -- {detail}" if detail else ""))
    return bool(condition)


def show(title, payload):
    print(f"    {title}: {json.dumps(payload)}")


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The dispatch harness (what Gemini CLI does between the model and the tool)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

class Dispatcher:
    """Drives hooks exactly as Gemini CLI's documented dispatch would and
    executes the surviving tool call. One instance carries the scratch-tree
    paths; `hook_log` is the ground truth of what actually routed to ICG."""

    def __init__(self, project, exec_log, hook_log, hook_env, exec_env):
        self.project = project
        self.exec_log = exec_log
        self.hook_log = hook_log
        self.hook_env = hook_env
        self.exec_env = exec_env

    @staticmethod
    def hook_matches(matcher, tool_name):
        """Gemini matcher semantics: a REGEX compared against the tool name
        (the docs' example is `read_.*` matching all file-reading tools),
        implemented as a search -- the permissive reading. An absent or
        empty matcher matches everything. ICG's installed matcher is
        anchored, so for it the search/full-match choice never differs."""
        if not matcher:
            return True
        return re.search(matcher, tool_name) is not None

    def run_hook(self, command, payload, timeout_ms):
        """Run one hook: payload on stdin, per-hook timeout in MILLISECONDS."""
        self.hook_log.append(command)
        try:
            proc = subprocess.run(
                command,
                shell=True,
                cwd=self.project,
                input=json.dumps(payload).encode(),
                capture_output=True,
                timeout=timeout_ms / 1000.0,
                env=self.hook_env,
            )
            return proc.returncode, proc.stdout.decode(errors="replace"), proc.stderr.decode(errors="replace"), False
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as expired:
            stdout = (expired.stdout or b"").decode(errors="replace")
            stderr = (expired.stderr or b"").decode(errors="replace")
            return None, stdout, stderr, True

    def dispatch(self, settings_file, payload, stand_in=None):
        """Select BeforeTool hooks from an installed settings.json by the
        documented matcher semantics, run the matches, merge their verdicts,
        and execute the surviving tool call.

        `stand_in` is the harmless command that stands in for a non-shell
        tool's execution (an MCP or file tool is not a shell command; the
        scenarios that use it prove routing, and the stand-in makes the
        admission observable).
        """
        config = json.loads(Path(settings_file).read_text())
        groups = config.get("hooks", {}).get("BeforeTool")
        if not isinstance(groups, list):
            raise SystemExit(f"{settings_file} carries no BeforeTool array")

        # The model's arguments, merged with (overridden field-by-field by)
        # every hookSpecificOutput.tool_input a hook returns -- the docs'
        # "merges with and overrides" semantics.
        merged_input = dict(payload["tool_input"])
        blocked = None
        runs = []
        for group in groups:
            matcher = group.get("matcher", "")
            if not self.hook_matches(matcher, payload["tool_name"]):
                continue
            for entry in group.get("hooks", []):
                if entry.get("type", "command") != "command":
                    continue
                rc, stdout, stderr, timed_out = self.run_hook(
                    entry["command"], payload, entry.get("timeout", 60000)
                )
                decision = interpret_before_tool(rc, stdout, stderr, timed_out)
                runs.append(
                    {
                        "command": entry["command"],
                        "matcher": matcher,
                        "exit": rc,
                        "timed_out": timed_out,
                        "stdout": stdout.strip(),
                        "decision": decision.as_dict(),
                    }
                )
                if not decision.proceeds:
                    blocked = decision
                elif decision.tool_input_override is not None:
                    merged_input.update(decision.tool_input_override)

        executed = None
        exec_rc = None
        if blocked is None:
            if payload["tool_name"] == "run_shell_command":
                executed = merged_input["command"]
                cwd = merged_input.get("directory") or payload.get("cwd") or self.project
                proc = subprocess.run(
                    executed,
                    shell=True,
                    cwd=cwd,
                    capture_output=True,
                    env=self.exec_env,
                )
                exec_rc = proc.returncode
            elif stand_in:
                executed = stand_in
                proc = subprocess.run(
                    stand_in, shell=True, capture_output=True, env=self.exec_env
                )
                exec_rc = proc.returncode
        return {
            "matched": len(runs),
            "runs": runs,
            "merged_input": merged_input,
            "blocked": blocked,
            "executed": executed,
            "exec_rc": exec_rc,
        }


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scratch-tree construction
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def write_executable(path, text):
    path.write_text(text)
    path.chmod(0o755)


def build_icg(repo_root, target_dir):
    env = dict(os.environ, CARGO_TARGET_DIR=str(target_dir))
    proc = subprocess.run(
        ["cargo", "build", "--release", "--locked"],
        cwd=repo_root,
        env=env,
    )
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        raise SystemExit(f"cargo build --release failed with exit {proc.returncode}")
    binary = target_dir / "release" / "icg"
    if not binary.exists():
        raise SystemExit(f"build reported success but {binary} is missing")
    return binary


def git_env(base_env):
    return dict(
        base_env,
        GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL="/dev/null",
        GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM="/dev/null",
    )


def make_git_fixture(scratch, base_env):
    """A diverged clone: the remote holds BASELINE, the clone holds DIVERGENT.

    Only a force-push could move the remote to DIVERGENT; a plain push is
    rejected as non-fast-forward. That asymmetry is what makes the rewrite
    substitution observable in S3.
    """
    remote = scratch / "remote.git"
    work = scratch / "work"
    env = git_env(base_env)
    identity = ["-c", "user.name=icg-e2e", "-c", "user.email=icg-e2e@invalid"]

    def git(*args, cwd=None):
        subprocess.run(
            ["git", *identity, *args],
            cwd=cwd or scratch,
            env=env,
            check=True,
            capture_output=True,
        )

    def head(cwd):
        return subprocess.run(
            ["git", *identity, "rev-parse", "HEAD"],
            cwd=cwd,
            env=env,
            check=True,
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
        ).stdout.strip()

    git("-c", "init.defaultBranch=main", "init", "--bare", str(remote))
    git("clone", str(remote), str(work))
    (work / "file.txt").write_text("baseline\n")
    git("add", "file.txt", cwd=work)
    git("commit", "-m", "baseline", cwd=work)
    git("push", "origin", "main", cwd=work)
    baseline = head(work)
    git("commit", "--amend", "-m", "divergent", cwd=work)
    divergent = head(work)
    assert baseline != divergent, "amend must produce a divergent commit"
    return remote, work, baseline, divergent


def rev_parse(cwd, ref, base_env):
    """Resolve a ref in a repository directory (work tree or bare)."""
    return subprocess.run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", ref],
        cwd=cwd,
        env=git_env(base_env),
        check=True,
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
    ).stdout.strip()


def exec_log_lines(exec_log):
    if not exec_log.exists():
        return []
    return [line for line in exec_log.read_text().splitlines() if line.strip()]


def seed_settings(project):
    """Unrelated settings and an unrelated event array, written BEFORE the
    installer runs -- the dispatch config must be the installer's MERGE
    output, and the unrelated values must survive it verbatim."""
    gemini_dir = project / ".gemini"
    gemini_dir.mkdir(parents=True)
    settings = gemini_dir / "settings.json"
    settings.write_text(
        json.dumps(
            {
                "model": "gemini-2.5-pro",
                "theme": "auto",
                "hooks": {
                    "AfterTool": [
                        {
                            "matcher": "",
                            "hooks": [{"type": "command", "command": "echo audit"}],
                        }
                    ]
                },
            },
            indent=2,
        )
        + "\n"
    )
    return settings


def run_installer(icg_bin, project, packs, base_env):
    return subprocess.run(
        [
            str(icg_bin),
            "install-gemini-hooks",
            "--project-dir",
            str(project),
            "--rule-pack",
            str(packs),
        ],
        cwd=project,
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        env=base_env,
    )


def payload_for(session_id, tool_name, tool_input, project, mcp_context=None):
    """The documented BeforeTool stdin payload."""
    doc = {
        "session_id": session_id,
        "transcript_path": f"/tmp/icg-gemini-e2e/{session_id}/transcript.json",
        "cwd": str(project),
        "hook_event_name": "BeforeTool",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-20T00:00:00Z",
        "tool_name": tool_name,
        "tool_input": tool_input,
        "original_request_name": tool_name,
    }
    if mcp_context is not None:
        doc["mcp_context"] = mcp_context
    return doc


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scenarios
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def scenario_stubs(scratch, project, dispatcher):
    """S0: prove the dispatch interpretation against stub hooks first, so the
    ICG scenarios are known to run through faithful semantics. None of these
    involve ICG; they pin the deny pair, the "block" alias, the permissive
    empty object, exit 2, exit 3, the millisecond timeout, unparsable
    stdout, the tool_input merge-override, and the two foreign-envelope
    controls that make ICG's `decision` spelling load-bearing."""
    print("\nS0  dispatch semantics, self-checked against stub hooks (no ICG)")
    stub_dir = scratch / "stubs"
    stub_dir.mkdir()
    stubs = {
        "deny.sh": "#!/bin/sh\nprintf '{\"decision\": \"deny\", \"reason\": \"rejected by policy\"}'\n",
        "alias.sh": "#!/bin/sh\nprintf '{\"decision\": \"block\", \"reason\": \"blocked by the alias\"}'\n",
        "empty.sh": "#!/bin/sh\nprintf '{}'\n",
        "sysmsg.sh": "#!/bin/sh\nprintf '{\"systemMessage\": \"heads up\"}'\n",
        "exit2.sh": "#!/bin/sh\nprintf 'rejected on stderr' >&2\nexit 2\n",
        "exit3.sh": "#!/bin/sh\nexit 3\n",
        "sleep.sh": "#!/bin/sh\nsleep 5\n",
        "garbage.sh": "#!/bin/sh\nprintf 'this is not json'\n",
        "merge.sh": "#!/bin/sh\nprintf '%s' \"$STUB_OVERRIDE\"\n",
        "cursor-shaped.sh": "#!/bin/sh\nprintf '{\"permission\": \"deny\", \"user_message\": \"foreign envelope\"}'\n",
        "claude-shaped.sh": "#!/bin/sh\nprintf '{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"permissionDecision\": \"deny\", \"permissionDecisionReason\": \"foreign envelope\"}}'\n",
    }
    for name, text in stubs.items():
        write_executable(stub_dir / name, text)
    merge_override = {
        "command": f"printf stub-override > {scratch}/markers/stub-merge",
    }

    def stub_settings(name, timeout_ms=60000):
        path = stub_dir / f"{name}.settings.json"
        path.write_text(
            json.dumps(
                {
                    "hooks": {
                        "BeforeTool": [
                            {
                                "matcher": INSTALLED_MATCHER,
                                "hooks": [
                                    {
                                        "type": "command",
                                        "command": str(stub_dir / f"{name}.sh"),
                                        "timeout": timeout_ms,
                                    }
                                ],
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                }
            )
        )
        return path

    marker = scratch / "markers" / "stub-open"
    base = payload_for(
        "stub-session",
        "run_shell_command",
        {"command": f"touch {marker}", "directory": str(project)},
        project,
    )
    cases = [
        # (stub, timeout_ms, stub exit to expect, expect_proceeds, label)
        ("deny", 60000, 0, False, "decision deny blocks and surfaces the reason"),
        ("alias", 60000, 0, False, 'the documented "block" alias blocks too'),
        ("empty", 60000, 0, True, "the permissive empty object proceeds"),
        ("sysmsg", 60000, 0, True, "a bare systemMessage proceeds (no decision attached)"),
        ("exit2", 60000, 2, False, "exit 2 blocks with stderr as the rejection reason"),
        ("exit3", 60000, 3, True, "exit 3 is a non-fatal warning and proceeds"),
        ("sleep", 300, None, True, "a timeout (measured in milliseconds here) is non-fatal and proceeds"),
        ("garbage", 60000, 0, True, "exit 0 with unparsable stdout proceeds (no fail-closed knob)"),
    ]
    for name, timeout_ms, expected_exit, expect_proceeds, label in cases:
        settings = stub_settings(name, timeout_ms)
        record = dispatcher.dispatch(settings, base)
        run = record["runs"][0]
        got = record["blocked"] is None
        detail = run["decision"].get("interpretation", "")
        ran_as_written = run["exit"] == expected_exit and (
            run["timed_out"] if expected_exit is None else not run["timed_out"]
        )
        if expect_proceeds:
            ok = ran_as_written and got and record["executed"] is not None
        else:
            ok = (
                ran_as_written
                and (not got)
                and record["executed"] is None
                and bool(record["blocked"].reason)
            )
        check(f"S0.{len(CHECKS)} {label}", ok, f"{detail} (exit={run['exit']})")

    # The tool_input merge-override: what the stub puts in
    # hookSpecificOutput.tool_input is what actually runs.
    write_executable(
        stub_dir / "merge.sh",
        f"#!/bin/sh\nprintf '%s' '{json.dumps({'hookSpecificOutput': {'tool_input': merge_override}})}'\n",
    )
    merge_marker = scratch / "markers" / "stub-merge"
    original = f"printf original > {merge_marker}"
    record = dispatcher.dispatch(
        stub_settings("merge"),
        payload_for(
            "stub-merge",
            "run_shell_command",
            {"command": original, "description": "original description"},
            project,
        ),
    )
    check(
        "S0.merge hookSpecificOutput.tool_input overrides the model's arguments",
        record["executed"] == merge_override["command"]
        and merge_marker.exists()
        and merge_marker.read_text() == "stub-override",
        f"executed {record['executed']!r}",
    )
    check(
        "S0.merge the merge keeps the fields the stub did not override",
        record["merged_input"]["description"] == "original description",
    )

    # Foreign-envelope controls: Gemini's hook reader acts on `decision`.
    # A Cursor-shaped or Claude-shaped deny on stdout carries no `decision`
    # key, so it proceeds -- if ICG rendered a foreign envelope here, its
    # denies would silently become allows.
    for name, envelope in (
        ("cursor-shaped", "Cursor's flat permission envelope"),
        ("claude-shaped", "Claude Code's nested permissionDecision envelope"),
    ):
        record = dispatcher.dispatch(stub_settings(name), base)
        check(
            f"S0.{name} a {envelope} is NOT a Gemini deny and proceeds",
            record["blocked"] is None,
            record["runs"][0]["decision"].get("interpretation", ""),
        )
    marker.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    merge_marker.unlink(missing_ok=True)


def scenario_install(scratch, project, packs, icg_bin, base_env):
    """The real installer produces the config every later scenario runs on."""
    print("\nS-install  the real installer wires the scratch project")
    settings = seed_settings(project)
    proc = run_installer(icg_bin, project, packs, base_env)
    check(
        "S-install install-gemini-hooks exits 0",
        proc.returncode == 0,
        proc.stderr.strip()[:200],
    )
    installed = json.loads(settings.read_text())
    groups = installed["hooks"]["BeforeTool"]
    check(
        "S-install exactly one BeforeTool group was added",
        len(groups) == 1,
        f"{len(groups)} groups",
    )
    group = groups[0]
    entries = group["hooks"]
    command = entries[0]["command"]
    check(
        "S-install the installed matcher is the anchored three-tool regex",
        group.get("matcher") == INSTALLED_MATCHER,
        group.get("matcher", ""),
    )
    check(
        "S-install the entry is a command hook with the bounded millisecond timeout",
        entries[0]["type"] == "command" and entries[0]["timeout"] == 10000,
        f"timeout={entries[0].get('timeout')!r}",
    )
    check(
        "S-install the entry invokes this icg binary for the gemini-cli harness with the shipped packs",
        command.startswith(f"'{icg_bin}' hook --harness gemini-cli --rule-pack {packs}")
        or command == f"{icg_bin} hook --harness gemini-cli --rule-pack {packs}",
        command,
    )
    check(
        "S-install unrelated settings and event arrays survive verbatim",
        installed["model"] == "gemini-2.5-pro"
        and installed["theme"] == "auto"
        and installed["hooks"]["AfterTool"]
        == [{"matcher": "", "hooks": [{"type": "command", "command": "echo audit"}]}],
    )
    check(
        "S-install the pre-install state was backed up once",
        (project / ".gemini" / "settings.json.icg-backup").exists(),
    )
    before = settings.read_text()
    again = run_installer(icg_bin, project, packs, base_env)
    check(
        "S-install re-running the installer is a byte-level no-op",
        again.returncode == 0
        and "already up to date" in again.stdout
        and settings.read_text() == before,
        again.stdout.strip().splitlines()[1][:80] if again.stdout else "",
    )
    print(f"    installed command: {command}")
    return settings


def scenario_deny(dispatcher, project, settings, scratch):
    """S1: the deny verdict stops the tool call -- the fake target never runs."""
    print("\nS1  BeforeTool DENY (openbao/openbao-inline-secret-literal)")
    deny_marker = scratch / "markers" / "deny"
    deny_command = (
        f"bao kv put secret/icg-gemini-e2e/demo password=hunter2 > {deny_marker} 2>&1"
    )
    payload = payload_for(
        "icg-gemini-e2e-session",
        "run_shell_command",
        {
            "command": deny_command,
            "description": "store a demo secret",
            "directory": str(project),
        },
        project,
    )
    show("payload", payload)
    log_before = exec_log_lines(dispatcher.exec_log)
    hooks_before = len(dispatcher.hook_log)
    record = dispatcher.dispatch(settings, payload)
    run = record["runs"][0]
    response = json.loads(run["stdout"])
    show("response", response)
    check(
        "S1 hook exited 0 with exactly the decision/reason pair",
        run["exit"] == 0
        and response.get("decision") == "deny"
        and set(response) == {"decision", "reason"},
        f"exit={run['exit']}, keys={sorted(response)}",
    )
    check(
        "S1 the reason is the tool error the agent would see and names the matched rule",
        "pack=openbao" in response.get("reason", "")
        and "openbao-inline-secret-literal" in response.get("reason", ""),
        response.get("reason", "")[:120],
    )
    check("S1 dispatch blocked the call", record["blocked"] is not None)
    check("S1 nothing was executed", record["executed"] is None)
    check("S1 marker file does not exist", not deny_marker.exists(), str(deny_marker))
    check(
        "S1 the bao binary was never invoked",
        not any(line.startswith("bao ") for line in exec_log_lines(dispatcher.exec_log)),
        f"exec log delta: {exec_log_lines(dispatcher.exec_log)[len(log_before):]}",
    )
    check(
        "S1 the hook routed exactly once (the matcher selected ICG for run_shell_command)",
        record["matched"] == 1 and len(dispatcher.hook_log) == hooks_before + 1,
    )
    return payload["session_id"]


def scenario_session_continues(dispatcher, project, settings, scratch, session_id):
    """S2: the denial is non-fatal for the CLI -- the same session's next call
    is still gated, passes on an allow, and executes."""
    print("\nS2  the session continues after the denial; an allow passes through")
    control_marker = scratch / "markers" / "control"
    follow_up = payload_for(
        session_id,  # the SAME session the S1 denial happened in
        "run_shell_command",
        {
            "command": f"touch {control_marker}",
            "description": "unremarkable follow-up call",
            "directory": str(project),
        },
        project,
    )
    show("payload", follow_up)
    hooks_before = len(dispatcher.hook_log)
    record = dispatcher.dispatch(settings, follow_up)
    response = json.loads(record["runs"][0]["stdout"])
    show("response", response)
    check(
        "S2 the follow-up call in the denied session is still gated",
        record["matched"] == 1 and len(dispatcher.hook_log) == hooks_before + 1,
        "the hook answered again after the denial",
    )
    check(
        "S2 the allow is the permissive empty object",
        response == {},
        "Gemini proceeds unless told otherwise; ICG never emits decision: allow",
    )
    check(
        "S2 the allowed command ran and created its marker",
        record["executed"] == f"touch {control_marker}"
        and record["exec_rc"] == 0
        and control_marker.exists(),
        f"executed {record['executed']!r}",
    )
    print(
        "    interpretation: the S1 reason reached the agent as the tool error --\n"
        "    the docs say that 'allow[s] it to respond or retry'; this follow-up\n"
        "    call in the same session is that retry, gated and admitted."
    )


def scenario_rewrite(dispatcher, project, settings, scratch, git_fixture):
    """S3: the rewrite's replacement input is what actually runs."""
    print("\nS3  BeforeTool REWRITE (git/git-force-push)")
    remote, work, baseline, divergent = git_fixture
    payload = payload_for(
        "icg-gemini-e2e-rewrite",
        "run_shell_command",
        {
            "command": "git push --force origin main",
            "description": "Push reviewed changes",
            "directory": str(work),
        },
        work,
    )
    show("payload", payload)
    git_before = [line for line in exec_log_lines(dispatcher.exec_log) if line.startswith("git ")]
    record = dispatcher.dispatch(settings, payload)
    run = record["runs"][0]
    response = json.loads(run["stdout"])
    show("response", response)
    nested = response.get("hookSpecificOutput", {})
    tool_input = nested.get("tool_input", {})
    check(
        "S3 hook exited 0 carrying only the hookSpecificOutput channel",
        run["exit"] == 0
        and set(response) == {"hookSpecificOutput"}
        and set(nested) == {"tool_input"},
        f"exit={run['exit']}, keys={sorted(response)}",
    )
    check(
        "S3 tool_input is the COMPLETE replacement input (every request field, only command substituted)",
        tool_input
        == {
            "command": "git push origin main",
            "description": "Push reviewed changes",
            "directory": str(work),
        },
        json.dumps(tool_input),
    )
    check(
        "S3 the documented merge-override yields the substituted command with the other fields intact",
        record["merged_input"]["command"] == "git push origin main"
        and record["merged_input"]["description"] == "Push reviewed changes"
        and record["merged_input"]["directory"] == str(work),
    )
    check(
        "S3 the merged input is what actually ran",
        record["executed"] == "git push origin main",
    )
    git_after = [line for line in exec_log_lines(dispatcher.exec_log) if line.startswith("git ")]
    new_git = [line for line in git_after if line not in git_before]
    check(
        "S3 the PATH-shim git observed exactly `push origin main`, never --force",
        new_git == ["git push origin main"],
        f"shim log delta: {new_git}",
    )
    check(
        "S3 the substituted plain push was rejected non-fast-forward "
        "(only the original could have moved the remote)",
        record["exec_rc"] not in (0, None),
        f"exec_rc={record['exec_rc']}",
    )
    check(
        "S3 the scratch remote still holds the baseline commit",
        rev_parse(remote, "refs/heads/main", dispatcher.exec_env) == baseline,
    )
    check(
        "S3 the clone still holds the divergent commit (nothing rewrote local state)",
        rev_parse(work, "refs/heads/main", dispatcher.exec_env) == divergent,
    )


def scenario_matcher_scoping(dispatcher, project, settings, scratch):
    """S4: an unmodeled/MCP tool name never routes to ICG at all."""
    print("\nS4  matcher scoping: unmodeled and MCP tool names never route to ICG")
    unmodeled = [
        "mcp__fs__write_file",
        "mcp_fs_write_file",
        "read_file",
        "google_web_search",
        "safe_write_file",
        "replace_all",
    ]
    for index, name in enumerate(unmodeled):
        marker = scratch / "markers" / "scoping" / str(index)
        is_mcp = name.startswith("mcp_")
        payload = payload_for(
            "icg-gemini-e2e-scoping",
            name,
            (
                {"filepath": str(marker), "content": "harmless"}
                if "write" in name
                else {"query": "harmless"}
            ),
            project,
            mcp_context={"server_name": "fs", "invocation_id": name} if is_mcp else None,
        )
        hooks_before = len(dispatcher.hook_log)
        record = dispatcher.dispatch(
            settings,
            payload,
            stand_in=f"touch {marker}",  # harmless stand-in for the tool's own execution
        )
        check(
            f"S4.{index} {name} selects ZERO hooks (ICG is never invoked)",
            record["matched"] == 0 and len(dispatcher.hook_log) == hooks_before,
            f"matched {record['matched']}",
        )
        check(
            f"S4.{index} {name} proceeds to execution untouched",
            record["executed"] is not None
            and record["exec_rc"] == 0
            and marker.exists()
            and record["blocked"] is None,
        )
        if is_mcp:
            check(
                f"S4.{index} {name} carries mcp_context and still routes nowhere",
                record["runs"] == [],
            )

    # Controls: each modeled name selects exactly the ICG hook.
    modeled_inputs = {
        "run_shell_command": {"command": f"touch {scratch}/markers/scoping/modeled-shell", "directory": str(project)},
        "write_file": {"file_path": f"{scratch}/markers/scoping/modeled-write", "content": "harmless"},
        "replace": {
            "file_path": f"{scratch}/markers/scoping/modeled-replace",
            "old_string": "a",
            "new_string": "b",
        },
    }
    for index, (name, tool_input) in enumerate(modeled_inputs.items()):
        payload = payload_for("icg-gemini-e2e-scoping", name, tool_input, project)
        hooks_before = len(dispatcher.hook_log)
        record = dispatcher.dispatch(
            settings, payload, stand_in=f"touch {scratch}/markers/scoping/modeled"
        )
        response = json.loads(record["runs"][0]["stdout"])
        check(
            f"S4.control {name} routes exactly once to the installed hook",
            record["matched"] == 1 and len(dispatcher.hook_log) == hooks_before + 1,
        )
        check(
            f"S4.control {name} is gated and passes unremarkable input",
            response == {} and record["blocked"] is None,
        )

    # The anchors are load-bearing: under the permissive search reading of
    # the docs' regex matchers, an UNANCHORED write_file would match the MCP
    # names and route them to ICG. The shipped anchors never do.
    for mcp_name in ("mcp__fs__write_file", "mcp_fs_write_file"):
        check(
            f"S4 an UNANCHORED write_file matcher would have routed {mcp_name} to ICG",
            re.search("write_file", mcp_name) is not None,
        )
        check(
            f"S4 the shipped anchored matcher never matches {mcp_name}",
            re.search(INSTALLED_MATCHER, mcp_name) is None,
        )


def scenario_hygiene(scratch):
    """S5: state hygiene -- verdict-shaped telemetry, the denial recorded with
    the command text redacted, and nothing written outside the scratch tree."""
    print("\nS5  hygiene")
    state = scratch / "state"
    telemetry = state / "telemetry.json"
    if telemetry.exists():
        text = telemetry.read_text()
        check(
            "S5 telemetry carries no command text (verdict-shaped only)",
            "hunter2" not in text and "--force" not in text,
        )
        try:
            store = json.loads(text)
            records = store["window"]["records"]
            check(
                "S5 telemetry records are verdict-shaped only",
                all(
                    set(record)
                    <= {"timestamp", "verdict", "release_ref", "session_id", "harness"}
                    for record in records
                ),
                f"{len(records)} records",
            )
            harnesses = {record.get("harness") for record in records}
            check(
                "S5 telemetry names the declared harness (gemini-cli)",
                "gemini-cli" in harnesses and harnesses <= {"gemini-cli"},
                f"harness values: {sorted(str(h) for h in harnesses)}",
            )
        except (json.JSONDecodeError, KeyError, AttributeError, TypeError):
            check("S5 telemetry parses into window.records", False, "unreadable telemetry store")
    else:
        check("S5 telemetry store exists in scratch", False, str(telemetry))
    denials = state / "denials.jsonl"
    if denials.exists():
        text = denials.read_text()
        check(
            "S5 the operational denial log recorded the denial with rule attribution",
            "openbao-inline-secret-literal" in text,
        )
        check(
            "S5 the denial log redacts the denied command text",
            "hunter2" not in text,
        )
    else:
        check("S5 denial log exists in scratch", False, str(denials))
    # Markers were confined to the scratch tree by construction; the caller
    # removes the whole tree on success.
    check(
        "S5 all markers lived under the scratch tree",
        all(str(path).startswith(str(scratch)) for path in (scratch / "markers").rglob("*")),
    )


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Entry point
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    parser.add_argument(
        "--repo-root",
        type=Path,
        default=Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent,
    )
    parser.add_argument("--icg-bin", type=Path)
    parser.add_argument("--keep", action="store_true")
    args = parser.parse_args()

    repo_root = args.repo_root.resolve()
    packs = repo_root / "packs"
    if not packs.is_dir():
        raise SystemExit(f"no packs directory under {repo_root}")

    scratch = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="icg-gemini-e2e.")).resolve()
    print(f"scratch tree: {scratch}")
    print(f"repo: {repo_root}")

    if args.icg_bin:
        icg_bin = args.icg_bin.resolve()
        print(f"using provided binary: {icg_bin}")
    else:
        build_dir = scratch / "target"
        print("building icg (cargo build --release --locked)...")
        icg_bin = build_icg(repo_root, build_dir)
        print(f"built: {icg_bin}")

    # Scratch layout.
    project = scratch / "project"
    project.mkdir()
    (scratch / "markers" / "scoping").mkdir(parents=True)
    (project / "bin").mkdir()
    state = scratch / "state"
    state.mkdir()
    home = scratch / "home"
    home.mkdir()
    exec_log = scratch / "exec-log"
    hook_log = []

    # PATH shims: the ground-truth record of what the dispatch actually
    # executed. `git` logs then execs the real binary; `bao` logs and
    # refuses, so even a gate failure could not reach a real OpenBao client.
    real_git = shutil.which("git")
    if real_git is None:
        raise SystemExit("git is required for the rewrite scenario")
    write_executable(
        project / "bin" / "git",
        f"#!/bin/sh\nprintf 'git %s\\n' \"$*\" >> {exec_log}\nexec {real_git} \"$@\"\n",
    )
    write_executable(
        project / "bin" / "bao",
        f"#!/bin/sh\nprintf 'bao %s\\n' \"$*\" >> {exec_log}\n"
        "printf 'bao shim: refusing to reach a real OpenBao client during the proof\\n' >&2\n"
        "exit 77\n",
    )

    # Every environment the hooks or the executed commands run under keeps
    # state inside the scratch tree and practice mode off (a practice flag
    # would convert denies to allows and hollow out the whole proof).
    base_env = dict(
        os.environ,
        HOME=str(home),
        ICG_PRACTICE="0",
        ICG_TELEMETRY_PATH=str(state / "telemetry.json"),
        ICG_DENIAL_LOG=str(state / "denials.jsonl"),
        ICG_STATE_PATH=str(state / "session-state.json"),
        ICG_HEALTH_PATH=str(state / "health-state.json"),
        GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL="/dev/null",
        GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM="/dev/null",
    )
    exec_env = dict(base_env, PATH=f"{project / 'bin'}:{os.environ['PATH']}")
    dispatcher = Dispatcher(
        project=project,
        exec_log=exec_log,
        hook_log=hook_log,
        hook_env=base_env,
        exec_env=exec_env,
    )

    settings = scenario_install(scratch, project, packs, icg_bin, base_env)
    git_fixture = make_git_fixture(scratch, base_env)
    scenario_stubs(scratch, project, dispatcher)
    session_id = scenario_deny(dispatcher, project, settings, scratch)
    scenario_session_continues(dispatcher, project, settings, scratch, session_id)
    scenario_rewrite(dispatcher, project, settings, scratch, git_fixture)
    scenario_matcher_scoping(dispatcher, project, settings, scratch)
    scenario_hygiene(scratch)

    failed = [label for label, ok in CHECKS if not ok]
    print(f"\n{len(CHECKS) - len(failed)}/{len(CHECKS)} checks passed")
    if failed:
        for label in failed:
            print(f"  FAILED: {label}")
        print(f"scratch tree left for diagnosis: {scratch}")
        return 1
    if args.keep:
        print(f"scratch tree kept (--keep): {scratch}")
    else:
        shutil.rmtree(scratch)
        print(f"scratch tree removed: {scratch}")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
